(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-reading-a-study-quiz"]={"title":"Reading a Study Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A survival study compared two treatments for a lymphoma. Patients given treatment A were mostly enrolled at diagnosis, while patients given treatment B were mostly enrolled after earlier therapy had failed. Treatment A showed far better survival. Which bias could falsely create this difference?","choices":["Migration bias","Susceptibility bias","Measurement bias","Publication bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the groups assembled for the study differ in their point in the disease course and in prior treatment, which alone can change survival. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"An abstract reads: \"We randomly assigned 600 adults with acute low back pain seen in primary care to naproxen or acetaminophen and measured disability scores at four weeks.\" Which detail is the outcome element of a matching clinical question?","choices":["Disability at four weeks","Adults with acute low back pain","Naproxen","Acetaminophen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the outcome is what the clinician hopes to measure, improve or affect, here disability scores at four weeks. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A rural clinician reading an abstract wants to know whether the study's participants and setting resemble her own patients before reading further. Which question of a cursory review is she asking?","choices":["Is it likely to be true?","To whom does it apply?","What was concluded?","How big was the effect?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because asking to whom the study applies directs the reader to the patients and setting, one step of a cursory review. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 41"},{"q":"A cohort study found that adults with gray hair had twice the rate of heart attack of adults without it. Gray hair was far more common in the older participants, and older age also raises the risk of heart attack. Which term best describes age in this study?","choices":["An effect modifier","The dependent variable","The independent variable","A confounding variable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because age is associated with both the exposure (gray hair) and the outcome (heart attack), so its effect cannot be separated from the effect of gray hair. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 37, 42; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 26, 28"},{"q":"During an outbreak, 200 people in a city of 100,000 were diagnosed with a new respiratory illness, and 10 of those 200 died of it. What is the case-fatality ratio?","choices":["0.01 percent","0.2 percent","5 percent","95 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the case-fatality ratio is the number of people who die divided by the number of people who have the disease: 10/200 = 5 percent. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A study of a home exercise program for heart failure enrolled volunteers who answered an advertisement placed in fitness centers. The comparison group was taken from a list of patients recently discharged from the hospital. The exercise group had fewer readmissions over a year. Which threat to validity is most likely?","choices":["Measurement bias","Attrition effect","Performance bias","Selection bias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the groups differ in more ways that affect the outcome than only the program, so few conclusions can be drawn about the program itself. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 40, 41, 44, 45"},{"q":"A clinician wonders whether vitamin D helps older people avoid falls. Which question is best formed for searching the literature?","choices":["In adults over 70, does vitamin D, compared with placebo, reduce falls?","In adults over 70, does daily vitamin D taken every winter reduce falls?","Does daily vitamin D, compared with a placebo tablet, reduce falls?","In adults over 70, is daily vitamin D better than a placebo tablet?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because it names all four parts: the patients (adults over 70), the intervention (vitamin D), the comparison (placebo) and the outcome (falls). — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 10"},{"q":"In a randomized trial of surgery versus medication, the coordinator enrolling patients could see the next assignment on an open list. Several frail patients were held back until a medication slot came up. Which safeguard was missing?","choices":["Allocation concealment","A placebo control","Stratified sampling","Exclusion criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because allocation concealment means the researchers assigning patients to treatment groups are blinded, so the coordinator could not have known the next assignment. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 15, 18, 21, 23"},{"q":"A hospital team identified 120 adults admitted with a first hip fracture and 240 adults of similar age admitted for planned surgery who had no fracture. Interviewers then asked everyone in both groups about long-term use of a sleeping medication during the previous five years. Which study design did the team use?","choices":["Retrospective cohort study","Case-control study","Prospective cohort study","Cross-sectional study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the groups were defined by the outcome (fracture or no fracture) and then assessed for a difference in past exposure, with the controls drawn from the hospital. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 17, 22, 27, 28; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A trial of an inhaled drug for chronic lung disease reports relative risks, drug versus placebo, with 95 percent confidence intervals: hospital admission 0.70 (0.55 to 0.90), death 0.85 (0.60 to 1.20), pneumonia 1.40 (0.90 to 2.20), and oral thrush 2.10 (1.40 to 3.10). Which outcome shows a statistically significant increase with the drug?","choices":["Pneumonia","Oral thrush","Death","Hospital admission"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because its whole interval lies above 1, the null value at which risk is equal in the two groups, so a true relative risk of 1 is not within the interval. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 14, 15; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A clinician looking into a treatment finds three sources: a case series of 12 patients, a cohort study of 5,000 patients, and a systematic review of eight randomized controlled trials. Which source sits highest in the hierarchy of evidence?","choices":["The cohort study","The case series","The systematic review","They rank equally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because systematic reviews sit at the top of the clinical studies pyramid, and studies at higher levels are least vulnerable to bias and more generalizable. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"Two drugs were tested for stroke prevention over five years. For drug A, the number needed to treat to prevent one stroke was 40, and the number needed to harm (major bleeding) was 25. For drug B, the number needed to treat was 120, and the number needed to harm was 400. Which statement best describes the two drugs?","choices":["B prevents more strokes and harms less often","A prevents more strokes and harms less often","B prevents more strokes but harms more often","A prevents more strokes but harms more often"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a smaller number needed to treat means a more effective intervention, while a smaller number needed to harm means harm occurs after fewer patients are treated. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 13, 14"},{"q":"A cohort study followed 400 adults who worked night shifts and 400 who worked only day shifts for five years. A peptic ulcer developed in 24 of the night-shift workers and 8 of the day-shift workers. What is the relative risk of ulcer for night-shift work?","choices":["0.04","0.33","3","6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because relative risk is the incidence in exposed persons divided by the incidence in non-exposed persons: 24/400 = 0.06 and 8/400 = 0.02, and 0.06/0.02 = 3. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 17, 18, 19, 24"},{"q":"A study of a knee examination maneuver for a torn ligament reports a sensitivity of 80 percent and a specificity of 90 percent. What is the positive likelihood ratio of the maneuver?","choices":["0.22","0.89","4.5","8"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the positive likelihood ratio is sensitivity/(1 - specificity) = 0.8/0.1 = 8, moderate evidence to rule the tear in. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 44"},{"q":"A health website about a joint supplement is written by a board-certified physician, gives detailed information on dosing and side effects, and was updated last month. The site is owned by the company that sells the supplement. Which element of the ABCD (Author, Bias, Content, Date) method raises the main concern?","choices":["Bias","Author","Content","Date"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a site owned by the seller of the product it describes raises the question of bias, which the author's credentials, the detail and the recent date do not answer. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 17, 18"},{"q":"A randomized trial of a migraine drug reports 1.5 fewer migraine days per month than placebo, with a p value of 0.08. How should this result be described?","choices":["Significant at the 0.05 level","Highly significant","Not statistically significant","Proof that the drug has no effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because p values less than 0.05 are generally accepted as statistically significant, and 0.08 is above that cutoff. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 11"},{"q":"Researchers compared each country's yearly sales of sugar-sweetened drinks per person with its rate of type 2 diabetes across 40 countries, using only national totals. No data on any individual person were collected. Countries with higher sales had higher diabetes rates. Which study design is this?","choices":["Meta-analysis","Case-control study","Ecologic study","Cohort study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a cross-sectional ecologic study relates the frequency of a characteristic to an outcome using population-level data, so applying its result to one person risks the ecologic fallacy. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 22, 25, 32"},{"q":"A pediatrician asks: \"In children with an ear infection, does a delayed antibiotic prescription, compared with an immediate antibiotic, relieve ear pain as well within three days?\" Which part of the question is the outcome?","choices":["Children with an ear infection","Relief of ear pain within three days","A delayed antibiotic prescription","An immediate antibiotic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the outcome is what the clinician hopes to accomplish, measure, improve or affect, here relief of ear pain within three days. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"An experienced cardiologist writes an overview of heart failure care, covering diagnosis, drugs, devices and end-of-life planning. She cites the trials she considers most useful, and the article describes no search method or rules for choosing studies. Which kind of article is this?","choices":["Systematic review","Meta-analysis","Narrative review","Cohort study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because it is an expert summary of evidence covering a broad topic and a range of issues, and its unstructured choice of cited articles is a recognized disadvantage. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 6, 22; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 3, 25"},{"q":"A company sponsored five trials of its new pain drug. The two trials that found a statistically significant benefit were published, while the three that found no significant difference were never submitted to a journal. A later review of the published trials concluded that the drug works. Which bias most affected that review?","choices":["Publication bias","Selection bias","Non-response bias","Confounding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because research without statistically significant results is less likely to be published, and a sponsor is less likely to support research that is unfavorable to its product. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 44, 46"},{"q":"A cohort study of 20,000 adults found that bladder cancer rates rose step by step with the number of cigarettes smoked per day, from light to moderate to heavy smokers. Which criterion for causality does this finding support?","choices":["Temporality","Reversibility","Dose-response","Consistency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a larger exposure leading to a higher rate of disease is the dose-response criterion for causality. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"On January 1, a survey of a retirement community of 2,000 residents found 160 with diabetes. Over the following year, 40 residents who had not had diabetes developed it. What was the prevalence of diabetes on January 1?","choices":["2 percent","8 percent","10 percent","20 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because prevalence is the portion of a group with a condition at a specific point in time: 160 of 2,000 residents is 8 percent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 15, 17, 20"},{"q":"A blood test for a liver disease was given to 1,000 adults, and a biopsy, the gold standard, showed that 50 of them had the disease. The test was positive in 40 of the 50 with the disease and in 95 of the 950 without it. An adult in this study tests positive. What is the probability that this adult has the disease?","choices":["30 percent","80 percent","90 percent","99 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because this is the positive predictive value, true positives divided by all positives: 40/(40 + 95) = 30 percent. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 16, 19, 24, 25"},{"q":"A trial of a new inhaler was run in ordinary primary care clinics, with patients managing their own doses, usual reminders and no extra monitoring, to learn how well it works in everyday practice. What does this trial mainly measure?","choices":["Efficacy","Non-inferiority","Effectiveness","Allocation concealment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because effectiveness represents real, ordinary circumstances and takes into account compliance, practicality and cost. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 27, 28, 29"},{"q":"A randomized trial tests whether a supervised exercise and education program, started after a first heart attack, reduces later complications such as heart failure. Which level of prevention does the trial study?","choices":["Secondary prevention","Primary prevention","Chemoprevention","Tertiary prevention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because tertiary prevention is clinical intervention to reduce complications following a diagnosis, and its treatments are usually studied with randomized trials. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 12"},{"q":"A trial of 40 patients followed for three months found no statistically significant difference in relapse between a new drug and placebo. A later trial of 2,000 patients followed for two years found a clear benefit of the same drug. What error did the first trial most likely make?","choices":["A type I error","Recall bias","Confounding","A type II error"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because concluding that no relationship exists when one does is a type II error, a false negative that can result from study limitations such as the length of follow-up. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 11, 34"},{"q":"A team wants to learn whether a medication taken during pregnancy raises the risk of a rare childhood cancer, diagnosed in about 1 in 50,000 children. Its budget covers a study of a few hundred children, and an answer is needed within about a year. Which study design best fits these limits?","choices":["Case-control study","Prospective cohort study","Randomized controlled trial","Cross-sectional study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because it starts from children who already have the cancer, so it needs smaller samples and no waiting for the condition to develop. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 15, 16, 29; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A randomized trial of a heart failure drug enrolled only men aged 40 to 60 with normal kidney function, at three academic medical centers, and found a clear benefit. A clinician in a community practice wants to give the drug to an 82-year-old woman with kidney disease. Which property of the trial is most in question for her?","choices":["Its internal validity","Its statistical significance","Its reliability","Its external validity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because external validity is the degree to which results are also true in other clinical settings, and this sample may not represent her. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 51; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A health department mailed a survey about exercise habits to 5,000 randomly chosen residents. Only 900 returned it, and most of those who replied were retired adults with more free time. The department reported that residents exercised far more than expected. Which threat to validity is most likely?","choices":["Attrition effect","Non-response bias","Measurement bias","Confounding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the people who responded differ from those who did not, so the results may not reflect the whole population. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 41, 42, 44"},{"q":"After reading an abstract, a reader wants to check how participants were assigned to groups and whether the outcome assessors were blinded. Which part of the article answers these questions?","choices":["The introduction","The methods","The discussion","The figures and tables"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because how strong the methods were is answered in the methods section during an in-depth review. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slides 39, 41"}]};
